{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And he shall bring it.</b> The vav and heh are pointed with segol." "<b>To the sons of Aaron.</b> In the courtyard, since he brings it to the courtyard. Fine flour that is not prepared does not require presentation to the altar, for it is self-evident that he brought it for the sake of Heaven. But those that come baked — such as pan, deep-pan, oven-baked, and gruel-cake — require presentation to the altar, in order to show that he baked them for the needs of the altar, so that it not appear like a person who prepares the entire meal for himself and afterward sends portions to his fellow." "<b>And he shall take a handful from there.</b> Rashi explained: this is a handful, according to its plain sense in the Hebrew language. Rashi explained thus: for we find elsewhere קומץ in the sense of a handful of reeds, in the language of Targum, as it is written ״באחת הפחתים״ and they translate ״בחדא מן קומציא״." "<b>The fullness of his handful.</b> How so? He covers three fingers over the palm of his hand and takes the handful. In the pan and in the deep-pan he wipes with his thumb above and with his little finger below, and this is one of the hard labors in the Temple."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {2 "The text appears to present Rashi's comment in two stages: first stating that קומץ means 'handful' in plain Hebrew, then substantiating it via the Targumic usage. The repetition of 'פרש״י' is preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:40.974154506Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}